from p4lib import Doc

d = Doc()
P = d.para

P("Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet} reports the mean raw (ungated) detection lead time",
  "On raw (ungated) lead, with 95\\% bootstrap CIs over the $n=3$ runs, the three deep reconstruction models top Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet} with 183--198~h, followed by the one-class SVM, with Hotelling $T^2$ leading the control charts; the intervals are wide at $n=3$, which is why the XJTU-SY, FEMTO and Ferrara campaigns follow. The false-alarm budget overturns this ordering: the daggered detectors post their long leads only by flooding the pre-onset region and have no valid operating point under $\\tau=10\\%$, leaving $3\\sigma$ and Deep SVDD as the deployable choices (Section~\\ref{sec:saxena} makes the reordering formal). The deep models score $L=0$ on all three IMS runs (valid-alarm fraction 0/3) because at the 97.5th-percentile threshold their pre-onset FAR ranges from 61\\% on test~3 to 100\\% on tests~1--2. Across the swept percentiles (Table~\\ref{tab:tradeoff}) TCN-AE and Transformer-AD never attain a valid operating point, while LSTM-AE attains one at the 99.5th percentile on test~3 alone (59.67~h of lead at 4.19\\% pre-onset FAR, unchanged across ten random seeds), leaving its valid-alarm fraction at 1/3; its pre-onset FAR averaged across the three runs, 62.00\\%, is why the across-run gate of Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet} scores it 0.0. Figure~\\ref{fig:health} shows the leakage-free onset and failure markers on a representative run, with the shaded training region from which the baseline statistics are computed.")

P("\\emph{Onset stability on IMS.}",
  "\\emph{Onset stability on IMS.} Across $k \\in \\{3,4,5\\}$ the default onset moves by less than two percent of run span on every run (Table~\\ref{tab:onset}). On the first IMS test it sits at ${\\sim}98\\%$ of span, only ${\\sim}12.9$~h before failure, so that run's ``pre-onset'' region is almost the entire record and may already contain early degradation, which explains the conformal result below.")
P("\\emph{Short-bearing onset stability (XJTU and FEMTO).}",
  "\\emph{Short-bearing onset stability.} Because the fixed-sample tolerances are a larger fraction of a sub-2-hour bearing than of a multi-week IMS run, we repeat the $k$ sweep on all sixteen short XJTU and FEMTO bearings. Every one yields an onset at the default $k$, including the shortest (XJTU Bearing2\\_4 at 42~min, FEMTO Bearing3\\_1 at 1.43~h), and the median across-$k$ spread is 1.0 percentage points of span on XJTU and 0.15 on FEMTO, versus 1.6 on IMS. The exceptions are XJTU Bearing1\\_2, which fires at only one of the three $k$ values, and FEMTO Bearing1\\_1, with the widest spread (8.9 points); both are short-lived bearings the per-bearing analysis already flags as hard.")
P("\\emph{Onset under a decoupled health indicator.}",
  "\\emph{Onset under a decoupled health indicator.} Because the default indicator shares its RMS/kurtosis basis with the detectors' features (Section~\\ref{sec:limitations}), we recompute the onset under a kurtosis-only indicator and under the first principal component of the health channels (Table~\\ref{tab:onset}, right). The onset is not invariant: on the slow-degrading test~2 a kurtosis-only onset fires at 98.8\\% of span against the default's 61.9\\%, because amplitude rises before impulsiveness there, so absolute lead magnitudes depend on the indicator; test~1 agrees across all three definitions (${\\sim}98$--99\\% of span). The headline is unaffected, because the aggregate-vs-decimate contrast uses raw lead time (Section~\\ref{sec:statproto}), which contains no onset term: re-running the full benchmark under the pca1 and kurt\\_only indicators leaves every raw-lead aggregate$-$decimate difference exactly unchanged, a maximum absolute deviation of 0.000~h across all 99 dataset $\\times$ detector $\\times$ indicator cells.")
P("\\emph{Alarm-persistence sensitivity.}",
  "\\emph{Alarm-persistence sensitivity.} Over persistence $\\in \\{1,3,5,10\\}$ windows (default 3; Table~\\ref{tab:robust}a) the median direction is stable but its run-level consistency is not: $3\\sigma$ has one negative run at the default ($n_+/n_-=2/1$, 3/0 elsewhere), EWMA is 2/1 at persistence 1, 3 and 5 and 3/0 only at 10, and Isolation Forest turns slightly negative at persistence 5. The $3\\sigma$ valid-alarm fraction stays 0.67 at full resolution but, pooled over the sampling sweep, falls from 0.67 to 0.33 by persistence 10, so a longer filter costs the deployable chart valid alarms at SCADA rates.")
P("\\emph{Missing-data (historian-gap) robustness.}",
  "\\emph{Missing-data (historian-gap) robustness.} Real historians drop records, so we set 5\\% and 20\\% of snapshot rows to missing (NaN, timestamp retained) and re-measure lead time on IMS and XJTU-SY over five independent draws per level (Table~\\ref{tab:robust}b), since a single random draw cannot separate a gap effect from the draw. At 20\\% every detector on both datasets stays inside the $\\pm 1$~h margin but one: the across-draw mean change is $+0.83$~h for $3\\sigma$ on IMS and $-0.04$~h on XJTU-SY, $-0.08$ and $-0.12$~h for EWMA, $-0.67$ and $-0.02$~h for CUSUM, and $-0.92$ and $-0.07$~h for Isolation Forest. The exception is Hotelling $T^2$ on IMS, which loses 51.2~h on average, bimodally across draws (0 to 128.1~h), according to whether a draw removes the rows carrying test~3's early alarm. It collapses in 2 of the 5 draws at 20\\%, giving an across-draw mean of 134.9~h over a range of 58.0--186.1~h, and the collapse is driven by a single run (test~3 falls from 315.9 to 59.7~h). At 5\\% missing, Hotelling $T^2$ and EWMA are unchanged in every draw, and no chart's per-draw mean lead moves by more than 0.83~h; an earlier single-draw run reported a 128.1~h collapse already at 5\\%, a property of that draw that we do not state. Valid-alarm fractions are unchanged on IMS (2/3 for every detector at every gap level, in every draw) and move by at most two bearings on XJTU-SY; apart from Hotelling $T^2$ on IMS at 20\\%, every cell of Table~\\ref{tab:robust}b varies across draws by less than 3.4~h on IMS and 0.37~h on XJTU-SY. We therefore claim gap-tolerance for the univariate charts and Isolation Forest---at most 1~h of lost warning at 20\\% missing rows on either dataset---and explicitly not for Hotelling $T^2$, whose full covariance estimate can lose far more on a short-runway dataset, draw-dependently at $n=3$.")

P("Ten run-to-failure bearings provide a far stronger test than IMS's three.",
  "On the ten XJTU-SY bearings the IMS ``aggregation helps'' trend does not replicate (Table~\\ref{tab:crossds}): the run-level median difference is 0~h for ten of the eleven detectors ($-0.10$~h for Isolation Forest), no sign test is significant ($p \\ge 0.18$) and no direction is consistent across bearings, while per-run differences stay within a few minutes, so aggregation does not destroy lead time either. Absolute leads are short (0--3.5~h) because the bearings are short-lived (52--533~min): over the five window-magnitude detectors ($3\\sigma$, EWMA, Hotelling $T^2$, Isolation Forest and the RMS-trend floor, as in Table~\\ref{tab:perbearing}), 209/450 evaluations yield a valid alarm, and the shortest-lived and abrupt-failure bearings are hard or impossible to warn on under any mechanism. Only the three longest-lived bearings form a length-30 sequence, so each deep sequence model is N/A in 76/90 cells, a consequence of short runs rather than tuning; where they run, their difference is null too. Halving the sequence length to 15 windows makes only one more short bearing usable, and the deep models still yield a valid alarm on at most one of the ten bearings. Figure~\\ref{fig:crossdataset} summarizes the cross-dataset picture: a positive but non-significant median shift on IMS that is not consistent across its three runs, and null effects on XJTU-SY, FEMTO, Ferrara and ONGC, every interval inside the $\\pm 1$~h margin.")
P("The six FEMTO Learning\\_set bearings provide a second",
  "All six FEMTO Learning\\_set bearings are long enough for every detector, including the deep reconstruction models, to run on the full $n=6$, and the verdict matches XJTU-SY (Table~\\ref{tab:crossds}): every run-level median difference is within $\\pm9$~min, only LSTM-AE and Transformer-AD are sign-consistent, both negative (0/3 and 0/4 non-zero runs), and the smallest sign-test $p$ is 0.125 (Transformer-AD, raw), which does not survive Holm correction. Across the three multi-bearing campaigns all 33 dataset $\\times$ detector cells are equivalent at $\\delta = 1$~h, with no interval endpoint reaching $\\pm 0.6$~h, so the margin is cleared with room to spare. Exactly five cells have an interval lying wholly below zero---Isolation Forest on XJTU-SY, and $3\\sigma$ (mean $-0.21$~h, $[-0.45,-0.04]$), LSTM-AE, RMS-trend and Transformer-AD on FEMTO---and every one favours decimation by less than the $\\pm 1$~h margin. These cells bound rather than overturn the conclusion: the direction of any residual effect is dataset-specific noise, while the replicated conclusion is non-destruction. Across all eleven detectors 211/660 FEMTO evaluations yield a valid alarm; as on XJTU-SY, the short lifetimes (${\\sim}1.4$--7.8~h) keep absolute lead times small.")
P("The University of Ferrara campaign \\cite{arpa2024ferrara} adds",
  "The University of Ferrara campaign \\cite{arpa2024ferrara}, a second independent constant-condition multi-bearing rig, gives the same verdict: every run-level median difference is within $\\pm5$~min, no detector is sign-consistent across the six bearings, and the smallest sign-test $p$ is 0.219 (CUSUM), not significant even before correction. Its 0.7--6.8~h lifetimes mean it cannot confirm or deny the IMS positive trend; it adds a second independent replication on a modern (2024) rig. The larger per-run negatives ($-0.5$ to $-1.1$~h) are all on E1, whose onset sits within ${\\sim}0.1$~h of failure (an abrupt, near-coincident-onset bearing like IMS test~1), not a systematic aggregation penalty.")
P("Figure~\\ref{fig:sweep} plots lead time against",
  "Under aggregation, IMS lead time stays high and stable as the logging interval coarsens; under decimation it degrades faster, with $3\\sigma$ and Isolation Forest collapsing at coarse intervals ($3\\sigma$ falls from 64.6~h at full rate to 24.8~h at $10\\times$ decimation while aggregation holds it near 64.6~h; Figure~\\ref{fig:sweep}). The upturn at the largest factor is the few-windows regime, where only one or two runs survive and the mean is no longer comparable; we annotate rather than hide it.")
P("Table~\\ref{tab:imsongc} gives the run-level test over all eleven",
  "At the run level (Table~\\ref{tab:imsongc}) the magnitude-monitoring detectors show a positive median difference, the largest for $3\\sigma$ ($+15.1$~h), but only CUSUM and Hotelling $T^2$ are sign-consistent: aggregation shortens the lead time of $3\\sigma$, EWMA and Isolation Forest on one run each, and Deep SVDD is positive in one run and tied in two. The three deep reconstruction models and the one-class SVM have negative medians, each with two negative runs and one positive, the deep models' positive run being test~3; the RMS-trend baseline is mixed. With $n=3$ the exact sign test cannot fall below $p=0.25$, a floor only CUSUM and Hotelling $T^2$ reach, so nothing is significant or survives Holm correction: the defensible reading is a null result with a positive median direction for the magnitude-monitoring detectors. The candidate mechanism is that bin-averaging acts as a low-pass filter suppressing single-window noise in the health statistic, so the alarm threshold is crossed earlier and held more persistently than under decimation; Appendix~D.1 tests it and Section~\\ref{sec:signaltheory} gives the signal-theoretic reason.")
P("The aggregate-vs-decimate question is asked once per detector",
  "The aggregate-vs-decimate question is asked once per detector on each inferential dataset, a family of $11 \\times 4 = 44$ tests (ONGC excluded) whose uncorrected family-wise error rate at $\\alpha=0.05$ is $1 - (1-0.05)^{44} \\approx 0.90$. Under Holm--Bonferroni no hypothesis survives (0 of 44): the smallest raw $p$ is 0.125 (Transformer-AD on FEMTO), and every one of the forty-four adjusted $p$-values is 1.00. After honoring the run structure and correcting for multiplicity there is no significant aggregate-vs-decimate effect on any dataset; the positive IMS median shift remains a trend that cannot be significant at $n=3$. The family is heterogeneous in power---the three deep sequence models contribute $n=3$ XJTU tests resting on the longest-lived, most warnable bearings ($\\ddagger$ in Table~\\ref{tab:crossds})---but every uncorrected $p$ already exceeds 0.1, so this changes no rejection decision; we flag it so $N=44$ is not read as forty-four equally powered tests.")
P("\\emph{Per-bearing heterogeneity.}",
  "\\emph{Per-bearing heterogeneity.} Table~\\ref{tab:perbearing} breaks XJTU-SY down by bearing at full resolution: the mean lead over the valid detectors is the realistic single-deployed-detector expectation, and the oracle ``best'' presumes foreknowledge of which detector fires first; they differ only where detectors disagree (Bearing2\\_2: 1.00~h mean vs.\\ 1.08~h oracle). The 209/450 valid-alarm figure hides strong heterogeneity. Long-degrading bearings such as Bearing2\\_3 (533~min life) and Bearing1\\_3 (158~min) are warnable with multi-hour or near-hour lead, whereas the two shortest-lived, abrupt-failure bearings, Bearing2\\_4 (42~min) and Bearing1\\_1 (123~min, sudden end), yield no valid alarm for any detector (2/10 bearings): their onset and failure nearly coincide, no method can manufacture warning time the physics does not provide, and the metric credits none.")
P("\\emph{The same contrast under the gated metric.}",
  "\\emph{The same contrast under the gated metric.} The headline uses raw lead, the quantity coarsening acts on and one independent of $\\tau$. Because we argue that ungated lead can mislead, we also run the contrast under the gated metric itself (Table~\\ref{tab:gatedcontrast}). The answer does not change: under either metric every inferential dataset's median run-level difference is at most a few thousandths of an hour, no detector is sign-consistent enough to reach significance, and no hypothesis survives Holm correction across the $N=44$ family. What changes is power: gating discards every run without a valid alarm, so the mean effective sample size per detector falls from 3.0 to 1.3 on XJTU-SY, 4.8 to 2.8 on FEMTO, 5.0 to 3.6 on Ferrara and 2.7 to 1.2 on IMS, roughly a halving. The gated contrast agrees with the raw one with materially less power, so raw lead stays the primary quantity and the gated version is a robustness check.")
P("Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}a plots the empirical pre-onset",
  "On IMS (Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}a, values in its caption) the distribution-free guarantee $\\text{FAR} \\le \\alpha$ holds on test~2 (0\\% through $\\alpha=0.10$) and on test~3 through $\\alpha=0.05$ (0.9\\% at $\\alpha=0.01$, 4.4\\% at 0.05), slightly exceeding the bound at $\\alpha=0.10$, where the empirical FAR is 13\\%. It fails on test~1, at 0.74--0.85 regardless of $\\alpha$, for the reason Table~\\ref{tab:onset} foretold: the onset fires only ${\\sim}12$~h before failure, so the ``pre-onset'' region already contains degradation and calibration and ``normal'' scores are not exchangeable. We keep this run because it delimits when the guarantee is usable in prognostics: it needs a genuinely stationary normal region, which an abrupt late-onset failure does not provide. On XJTU-SY (Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}b) bearings with a substantial stationary pre-onset region (e.g.\\ Bearing2\\_3, calibrated through $\\alpha=0.05$) honor the bound, while those with only one or two pre-onset windows, an artifact of short lifetimes, are flagged uncalibrated rather than silently passed. The conformal machinery thus doubles as a per-asset usability test of whether enough stationary normal data exists to control the false-alarm rate at all.")
P("\\emph{FEMTO and ONGC calibration.}",
  "\\emph{FEMTO and ONGC calibration.} On FEMTO (Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}c) no bearing honors the bound at $\\alpha=0.05$ (pre-onset FAR 0.08--0.56; pooled 0.27): the short 1.4--7.8~h lifetimes leave a pre-onset region that is neither long nor stationary. The most stationary bearings come closest (Bearing2\\_2 at 0.08, Bearing1\\_2 at 0.10) and the abrupt Bearing1\\_1 (0.56) violates it badly. On the real ONGC historian stream (Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}d) the calibration tracks the diagonal closely (empirical FAR 0.016, 0.030 and 0.096 at $\\alpha=0.01$, 0.02, 0.05), a modest 1.5--2$\\times$ above target, consistent with a mostly-stationary record that only mildly violates exchangeability. The guarantee is therefore usable where a stationary normal region exists (ONGC, the longer IMS/XJTU bearings) and self-reports as invalid where it does not (short FEMTO/XJTU bearings, abrupt onsets).")
P("Sweeping the alarm-threshold percentile traces",
  "Sweeping the alarm-threshold percentile traces a prognostics analog of the ROC curve (Figure~\\ref{fig:tradeoffs}, Table~\\ref{tab:tradeoff}): tightening the threshold lowers pre-onset FAR and lead together, letting an operator select an operating point for a stated false-alarm budget. On the achievable frontier (highest lead at the lowest attainable FAR) Hotelling $T^2$ and Isolation Forest dominate the upper left, while the naive RMS-trend baseline collapses to near-zero lead once the threshold tightens (1.3~h at the 99.5th percentile). Many operating points exceed the $\\tau=10\\%$ budget and are invalid ($\\dagger$). Hotelling $T^2$ has the highest raw lead of any control chart but exceeds the budget at every threshold tested, so it has no valid operating point on IMS; EWMA and CUSUM behave the same way, their pre-onset FAR sitting at 19.0--22.1\\% across the swept thresholds, so neither memory-based chart should be recommended under a 10\\% budget. Deep SVDD occupies the opposite corner, within budget ($\\le 0.6\\%$ FAR) only by firing late ($\\le 14.8$~h of lead). $3\\sigma$ reaches a valid point, 56.3~h of lead at 8.9\\% FAR at the 99.5th percentile, and is the recommended chart under a 10\\% budget---a ranking the raw-lead column of Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet}, led by the deep models, would by itself obscure. The ten XJTU bearings (Figure~\\ref{fig:tradeoffs}b) give the same qualitative shape at shorter absolute leads, so the operating-point selection generalizes beyond the laboratory rig.")
P("\\emph{Sensitivity to the false-alarm budget.}",
  "\\emph{Sensitivity to the false-alarm budget.} The budget $\\tau$ is a policy input, and the recommendation is not $\\tau$-invariant (Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet}). At $\\tau=0.20$ the $3\\sigma$ chart gives the longest valid lead (77.0~h), joined by CUSUM, Hotelling $T^2$ and Isolation Forest as their pre-onset FAR falls within the looser budget; at $\\tau=0.10$ it remains the best valid chart (56.3~h); at a strict $\\tau=0.05$ no control chart qualifies, and only Deep SVDD (14.8~h) and the RMS-trend floor (34.7~h) stay within budget, so an operator with very low nuisance-alarm tolerance should prefer the low-FAR Deep SVDD point. The $3\\sigma$ recommendation therefore holds for $\\tau \\ge 0.10$ on IMS and inverts toward the low-FAR detectors below it. The cross-dataset headline, computed on raw lead-time differences (Section~\\ref{sec:statproto}), does not depend on $\\tau$ at all.")
P("\\emph{Where does $\\tau=10\\%$ come from? A cost-ratio argument.}",
  "\\emph{Where does $\\tau=10\\%$ come from?} Consider an expected-cost model with two site parameters: $c_I$, the cost of responding to one nuisance alarm (a wasted inspection), and $c_F$, the cost of an unplanned bearing failure (downtime plus secondary shaft/housing damage). Over a pre-onset life of $k$ windows a detector at pre-onset FAR $f$ issues ${\\approx} kf$ nuisance inspections, and a valid alarm with adequate lead converts the failure into a planned repair, saving $c_F$. False alarms are worth tolerating up to the budget at which their cumulative cost equals the warning's value, $k\\tau c_I \\approx c_F$, i.e.")
P("For rolling-element bearings an unplanned failure typically costs",
  "For rolling-element bearings an unplanned failure typically costs $\\rho = 10^2$--$10^4$ times a routine inspection, and the pre-onset window count is of order $k \\sim 10^3$ on these runs, so Eq.~(\\ref{eq:costratio}) places the break-even budget at $\\tau^* \\sim 0.1$--1 ($\\tau^*$ saturates at 1 for $\\rho \\ge 10^3$ and falls to 0.10 at $\\rho=10^2$, $k=10^3$). Our $\\tau=0.10$ is the conservative end, justified whenever failures are at least ${\\sim}100\\times$ costlier than inspections; the strict $\\tau=0.05$ column corresponds to inspections that are themselves disruptive (e.g.\\ requiring a shutdown), a small $\\rho$, exactly where the low-FAR Deep SVDD point becomes economical. This is an order-of-magnitude anchor, not a site-specific optimisation: the true optimum needs an operator's actual $c_I$ and $c_F$, which we do not have, and Eq.~(\\ref{eq:costratio}) lets a practitioner substitute their own.")
P("We ablate feature families on IMS",
  "On IMS, time-domain amplitude features (rms\\_only or time\\_only) give each of the six non-sequence detectors its highest or tied-highest validity, and adding spectral, envelope or all features never improves validity for any of them (Table~\\ref{tab:ablation_general}). For the SPC charts the effect is sharp: rms\\_only is the unique group at which $3\\sigma$ and EWMA reach full validity (1.00 at 0\\% pre-onset FAR), while spectral, envelope and all collapse all four charts to 0.33 by driving their pre-onset FAR to 42--63\\%, the variance-sensitive charts reacting violently to noisy extra dimensions. Deep SVDD fails the opposite way: its pre-onset FAR stays near 0\\% under every feature set, yet spectral features cut its validity to 0.33 (0.00 for spectral\\_only) by delaying or suppressing the alarm---missed detections, not false alarms. The recommendation is detector-general on IMS: under a false-alarm budget, restrict every detector to time-domain amplitude features and admit frequency-domain features with care, most of all for the SPC charts. Raw lead inverts this ranking without overturning it: on Isolation Forest the envelope group gives the longest mean lead (99.4~h) and rms\\_only the shortest (32.0~h), but the long-lead groups accrue 23--29\\% pre-onset FAR against 0.2\\% for rms\\_only, buying lead by firing indiscriminately; per run, spectral features flip $3\\sigma$, EWMA and Hotelling $T^2$ from valid to invalid on test~2 at 100\\% pre-onset FAR while Isolation Forest stays valid in both settings. Repeated at every coarsening factor, rms\\_only stays uniquely best at native and $2\\times$ resolution ($f \\le 2$), matching the argument that bin-averaging preserves the amplitude statistic; at $f \\ge 5$ only a handful of windows survive per run, valid fractions move in steps of 1/3, and whether the rms\\_only advantage holds at the coarsest SCADA rates is not resolvable at $n=3$.")
P("If the false-alarm gate is what separates a usable warning",
  "If the false-alarm gate is what separates a usable warning from a manufactured one, a metric without it should reward detectors ours rejects. We test this against the most widely used ungated warning-time metric, the Saxena prognostic horizon (PH) \\cite{saxena2008metrics,saxena2010metrics}: $\\text{PH} = \\max(0, (t_f - \\text{FAT})/3600)$~hours, with no false-alarm constraint. It is exactly our lead time $L$ before the validity gate (Eq.~\\ref{eq:valid}) is applied, and that one missing conjunct is decisive.")
d.drop("The PH is the interval between a detector's first alarm and failure")
P("\\emph{PH is maximized by the latch-on detector.}",
  "\\emph{PH is maximized by the latch-on detector.} On the three IMS runs the constant-on detector earns the full run length as PH (164--1073~h) while flooding the pre-onset region ($\\text{FAR}_{\\text{pre}} \\approx 1$), so the gated metric scores it $L=0$ on every run; the onset oracle is the mirror image, with zero pre-onset false alarms and the maximum achievable lead (12.9, 62.5 and 44.5~h).")
P("\\emph{PH and $L$ disagree on the real ranking.}",
  "\\emph{PH and $L$ disagree on deployable detectors too.} Ranked at their own best operating points (Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet}), the two orders nearly invert at the top. The seven detectors PH ranks highest---LSTM-AE 199.6~h, TCN-AE 185.4~h, Transformer-AD 184.0~h, the one-class SVM 180.2~h, Hotelling $T^2$ 176.9~h, Isolation Forest 174.7~h and EWMA 89.1~h---have no valid operating point under a 10\\% false-alarm budget, so their gated lead is $L=0$; the deep models alarm through 61--100\\% of every pre-onset region. $3\\sigma$, which PH ranks only eighth (77.0~h raw), is the one that clears the budget (56.3~h of lead at 8.9\\% pre-onset FAR) and the correct recommendation; CUSUM (65.0~h, PH's ninth) also scores $L=0$, and Deep SVDD, ranked last by PH, rises to a valid 14.8~h.")
P("\\emph{Implication.}",
  "\\emph{Implication.} Any warning-time metric that does not gate on $\\text{FAR}_{\\text{pre}}$ can be driven to its maximum by loosening the alarm threshold, in the limit to the constant-on detector; ours closes this by construction and PH does not. On IMS this changes the recommended detector from the ones PH ranks first by raw lead to $3\\sigma$, the only classical chart with a valid operating point at a 10\\% budget: PH rewards precisely the detectors a false-alarm-budgeted operator must avoid.")
P("A practitioner commissioning condition monitoring",
  "How much normal run-in history must a new asset collect before a deep model can be trusted over a simple control chart? We sweep the training fraction $T \\in \\{0.20, \\dots, 0.60\\}$ on FEMTO, the one dataset where all six bearings are long enough for every detector, including the seq-len-30 deep models (calibration fixed at 0.10, test $= 1-T-0.10$, no per-$T$ retuning). At $T=0.20$ the shortest bearing (Bearing3\\_1) forms only 20 training windows, fewer than the 30 a sequence requires, so LSTM-AE, TCN-AE and Transformer-AD are explicit N/A there (Section~\\ref{sec:detectors}); these are the only three N/A cells, the other 297 of the 300 (detector, bearing, $T$) cells being genuine evaluations. Valid-alarm fractions use the full $n=6$ with an N/A counted as no valid alarm, the conservative choice; the released sweep records the N/A count separately.")
P("The result (Figure~\\ref{fig:mintrain}) is unambiguous",
  "There is no crossover (Figure~\\ref{fig:mintrain}). The four SPC charts are at or near their best at the \\emph{smallest} training fraction---$3\\sigma$ at 1.00, EWMA and Hotelling $T^2$ at 0.667, CUSUM at 0.500---and more commissioning data does not improve them: $3\\sigma$ falls to 0.833 at $T=0.30$ and 0.667 from $T=0.40$ on, Hotelling $T^2$ falls to 0.500, EWMA is flat at 0.667, and CUSUM is flat at 0.500 apart from a one-bearing rise to 0.667 at $T=0.50$. Isolation Forest starts and ends at 0.667 but dips to 0.167 at $T=0.30$; only the naive RMS-trend baseline rises with $T$, from a far lower base (0.000 to 0.500). This is a consequence of the design, not reverse data-hunger: the test fraction $1-T-0.10$ shrinks as $T$ grows, so on bearings this short a better-estimated baseline comes with a shorter window in which to use it. The three deep reconstruction models sit far below the charts at every training fraction (LSTM-AE and Transformer-AD 0.000--0.333, TCN-AE 0.000--0.167) and do not close the gap. Deep SVDD has no valid alarm below $T=0.60$ and 0.167, one bearing, at $T=0.60$, the shortest test window: as on IMS test~1 it fires very late, because its threshold requires substantial encoder-distance growth from the trained center, so on these 1.4--7.8~h bearings its first alarm arrives at or after failure on all six bearings below $T=0.60$ and on five of the six at $T=0.60$. That is a bearing-lifetime constraint, not a training-data constraint.")
P("The practical recommendation is therefore narrower",
  "On bearings of the lifetimes tested here, control charts should therefore be preferred regardless of how much run-in history is available, because the deep reconstruction models do not overtake them anywhere in the $[0.20, 0.60]$ training range. This is a statement about a data regime, not the architectures: across bearings and training fractions these runs furnish between 20 and 335 normal training windows, whereas the models carry $2.0$--$5.5 \\times 10^{4}$ parameters, so the comparison is made where deep models are structurally disadvantaged. Whether a crossover appears on genuinely long-running assets, where a deep model could see orders of magnitude more normal windows, is the central open question (Section~\\ref{sec:limitations}).")
d.save()
